// Flows that answer a query, and the ways an answer arrives smaller than asked.
//
// One read lock spans the whole pipeline, so the two legs can never read
// different generations. Everything that can go wrong is either an explicit
// degradation carrying its reason or a typed refusal — never a silently empty
// result.

views {

  dynamic view searchRequest {
    title 'Hybrid search request'
    description '''
      The read lock is taken after the worktree is resolved and released only
      after the results are shaped — it prevents the two legs from reading
      different generations. An unsupported mode is refused before resolution
      and before any lock. If the lexical head does not validate, the answer is
      an explicitly degraded dense_only with the reason stated; if both legs are
      unavailable it is INDEX_UNAVAILABLE, never an empty success.
    '''

    claudeCode -> localRag.mcpProxy 'tools/call search_code'
    localRag.mcpProxy -> localRag.daemon.mcpDispatcher 'request envelope with the explicit context'
    localRag.daemon.mcpDispatcher -> localRag.daemon.searchEngine 'dispatch'
    localRag.daemon.searchEngine -> localRag.daemon.lockRegistry 'take the worktree read lock, bounded wait'
    localRag.daemon.searchEngine -> localRag.stateDb 'resolve the active generation and model space'
    localRag.daemon.lexicalLeg -> localRag.cacheDb 'BM25, only if the FTS head validates'
    localRag.daemon.denseLeg -> localRag.daemon.providerPool 'embed the query'
    localRag.daemon.denseLeg -> localRag.projectionStore 'brute-force cosine over the shard'
    localRag.daemon.fusion -> localRag.daemon.enrichment 'reciprocal rank fusion, k = 60'
    localRag.daemon.enrichment -> localRag.stateDb 'cut snippets from the stored source_blob'
    localRag.daemon.searchEngine -> localRag.daemon.lockRegistry 'release the read lock'
    localRag.daemon.mcpDispatcher -> localRag.mcpProxy 'results, generation, degraded flag, diagnostics'
    localRag.mcpProxy -> claudeCode 'tool result'

    global style status
  }

  dynamic view searchDegradation {
    title 'Search — the ways an answer arrives smaller'
    description '''
      Every reduction is explicit and carries its reason, so an acceptance test
      can assert WHY rather than only that something was missing. Note what is
      NOT a degradation: a single-leg mode that served is not degraded, because
      degraded means "less than you asked for"; when its one leg cannot serve,
      the answer is a refusal instead. Nothing on this path repairs anything —
      the shard the request opens is given a vector source that never answers,
      which is how "no tool call performs synchronous indexing" becomes a
      property of the type rather than a rule to remember.
    '''

    claudeCode -> localRag.daemon.mcpDispatcher 'search_code'
    localRag.daemon.mcpDispatcher -> localRag.daemon.searchEngine 'mode=semantic: UNSUPPORTED_MODE, before resolution and before any lock'
    localRag.daemon.searchEngine -> localRag.stateDb 'no active tuple: WORKTREE_NOT_INDEXED, before either leg'
    localRag.daemon.searchEngine -> localRag.daemon.lockRegistry 'read lock wait exhausted: BUSY_RETRY — the only retryable code'
    localRag.daemon.ftsMaterializer -> localRag.daemon.searchEngine 'head invalid: degraded dense_only, the leg is not run at all'
    localRag.daemon.shardManager -> localRag.daemon.searchEngine 'shard unavailable: degraded lexical_only, no internal retry'
    localRag.daemon.searchEngine -> localRag.daemon.mcpDispatcher 'both legs down: INDEX_UNAVAILABLE with both diagnostics — never an empty success'
    localRag.daemon.mcpDispatcher -> claudeCode 'isError content carrying the code, never a bare JSON-RPC -32603'

    global style status
  }
}
